## Checks

```bash
scripts/check.sh
```

The full check runs, in this order, the project's code checks (`scripts/checks.d/`, through
`scripts/check-project.sh`), the link check with its check of backticked code paths
(`scripts/check-docs.sh`), the decision records (`scripts/check-decisions.sh`), and
the template integrity (`scripts/check-template.sh`). It runs every part even when an earlier one
fails; a passing part prints one line, a failing part the end of its output, and `--verbose`
prints everything. `scripts/check.sh --if-changed` returns at once when nothing changed since the
last passing run. CI runs the same check in the `check` workflow.

Requires Git, bash, and Node.js 20 or later; nothing else to install. External links are not
checked; a project that wants it adds a check such as [lychee](https://lychee.cli.rs/) in
`scripts/checks.d/`.

## Pull requests

`/create-pr` in Claude Code opens the pull request: it runs the full check, reviews the change by
the class of its paths (`.claude/path-classes`), writes the description, and watches the CI
checks. `--skip-review` skips the review, for a caller that reviewed the change itself;
`--skip-watch` skips the watch of the CI checks.

## Modules

The answers in `.afe-answers.json` turn these on or off:

- `code_review` (default off): Claude also reviews each pull request that changes code in CI; it
  needs the Claude GitHub App and the `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` secret.
- `secret_scan` (default on): the `secret-scan` workflow scans pull requests and pushes to `main`
  for leaked secrets.
- `acceptance_check` (default on): readiness requires a test for every identifier a pull request
  lists under "Acceptance and test plan" (`TEST_GLOBS` in `.claude/harness.conf`).
- `fail_without_change` (default off): in CI only, every new or changed test must fail on the
  base branch (`FWC_TEST` in `.claude/harness.conf`).

## Protected main branch

Changes reach `main` only through pull requests with passing checks, merged with a merge
commit. After the first push, a repository admin applies the repository settings and the GitHub
ruleset once (`--dry-run` shows them first): merge commits only and "Automatically delete head
branches", on every plan; secret scanning with push protection, which a private repository may
need GitHub Advanced Security for; and a ruleset on `main` that requires a pull request, allows
no deletion or force push, and requires the checks `check` and `integrity`{% if fail_without_change %} (and, once
set as below, `base`){% endif %}, and only these.
Rulesets need a public repository or, for a private one, a paid plan.

```bash
scripts/protect-branch.sh
```
{%- if fail_without_change %}

Once the `base` job of the `fail-without-change` workflow passes in CI on a pull request that
changes a test, not as a SKIP, run it again with `--require-base` to require that job too; the
label `tests-pass-on-base` still lets a test that passes on the base through.
{%- endif %}

